WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.320
- Okay, something I just
learned about you is

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:04.050
that you consider yourself an HSP.

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:05.170
What's an HSP?

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:08.760
- Oh, so I actually, this is a,

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.390
it stands for highly sensitive person.

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:12.050
I didn't know it was a thing.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:14.060
I'm just like the way that I am.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:18.460
Right, so apparently there's
a diagnosis they give you.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:20.010
It's called a highly sensitive person.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:21.750
And this actually came up.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.650
So I'm starting a mental health podcast.

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:25.400
We're launching second week of January.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:26.730
So right after "Call Me Kat",

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.810
I have a podcast called
"Mayim Bialik's Breakdown",

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.840
and we're, get it?

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.230
So I can say, "Welcome to my breakdown."

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:33.700
That's literally why we named it that.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:34.840
But it's all about mental health.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:36.530
It's all about all the things about us.

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:38.370
Not just what medication
you're supposed to take,

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.090
and what your life should look like,

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.480
it's real people talking
about their experience

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.313
with mental health.

00:00:43.313 --> 00:00:44.170
I talk about my experiences.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:47.780
So one of the things about
me is that I'm extremely,

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:49.280
I've always been called sensitive.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.610
I've always been called hypersensitive.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.280
And I was like, "You're just
trying to keep me down."

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:53.957
But it was someone we had
on my podcast who was like,

00:00:53.957 --> 00:00:55.207
"Oh, it's called an HSP."

00:00:55.207 --> 00:00:57.560
And I was like, "Now I have a diagnosis."

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.030
And it's a diagnosis for people

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:01.140
who feel things very, very deeply.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.110
Sometimes they call us intuitives.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:05.090
Sometimes they call
children Indigo children

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:05.923
if they're like this.

00:01:05.923 --> 00:01:07.830
I'm like, I'm a hippie,
but that's too hippie.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:09.870
Turns out it's not,
'cause I think it's true.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.473
- I know, it sounds so
hippy-dippy but you're right.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:16.070
- Science has caught up
with thousands of years

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.350
of Eastern medicine, of meditation.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:20.790
Those things have science to them,

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:23.170
and that's what I'm trying
to break down for people.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:27.520
This is where I wanna put
my time and my neuroscience.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.190
- Well, I'm gonna tell you it's
crazy for me looking at you.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.664
I feel like you have it all.

00:01:32.664 --> 00:01:33.660
- No!

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:34.690
Oh my goodness!

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:35.970
- Beautiful, you're funny.
- Stop it.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:36.990
- No, I'm just saying though.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.890
It's important to point out
because we look at people

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.940
and we go, "Well, they probably,
they don't suffer as much

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:45.360
'cause they've got all
their cards together.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:46.870
They got all their,"
I can't say that word.

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:49.500
I'm trying to think of another
word other than that (beep).

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.660
- No, but that's something

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:53.610
that I actually have sort of dived into,

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:54.840
especially with this podcast.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:55.730
I have people on

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:58.670
who are far more outstanding,
attractive, successful.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:01:59.930
And I'm like, "How do you do it?"

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:01.350
And you know what?

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.690
They're just like anyone
who wants to look deeply

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:06.130
at why their life isn't working

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:06.963
or why they don't feel good.
- But I think that makes

00:02:06.963 --> 00:02:08.040
other people feel normal.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:09.020
- Exactly.
- Yeah.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:09.853
'Cause we all-

00:02:09.853 --> 00:02:11.100
- It sounds like my mother sent you a text

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.430
and was like, "Tell her she's beautiful

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:13.263
and she's got everything."

00:02:13.263 --> 00:02:14.760
- I just love every time you're talking,

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:16.589
it comes in this voice.

00:02:16.589 --> 00:02:17.880
- It's my favorite part.
- That's my mom's voice.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:18.860
- Is it? I love that.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:20.220
I love your mom already.

